== Architecture ==
{{Main|Architecture of Balisca}}

The architectural focus of this period was firstly on revivals and {{W|Eclecticism in architecture|eclecticism}}, and later on the creation of a "cosmopolitan" approach, drawing from across the confederation, and reflecting the [[Ethnography of Balisca|country's ethnography]].

The 1838 inauguration of [[Michelangelo Valentino]] and the new progressive ''[[Anyos Federales]]'' also embraced this emerging "cosmopolitan style", and it rapidly rose in popularity. It is characterized by its mix of decorative elements from various traditions—Neo-Mozbayani, Mozbayan Revival, and [[W:Palazzo style|Palazzo style]]—and widespread use of {{W|Architectural terracotta|architectural ceramics}} and oriental motifs. The arrival of {{W|Modernisme}} to the Baliscan academic arena was relatively quick due to the emerging cultural and historical ties to the [[Mesogean world]], and heavily influenced the eventual development of the [[New Cosmopolitan style]]. During the later years of the 19th century, there was a substantial interest in {{W|Moorish Revival architecture}}.

=== Cosmocazo/Cosmopolitan Deco ===
{{Main|Architecture of Balisca#Cosmocazo Deco|Edificio Kayani|Baliscan socialist realism}}

The ''Cosmocazo Deco'' (or Cosmopolitan Deco) was the skyscraper vocabulary of the late Cosmocazo period, emerging in the 1910s as the [[Baliscan Gilded Age|Gilded Age]] entered its most competitive phase. It was the Baliscan variant of the international [[W:Art Deco|Art Deco]] movement that originated in [[Volisania]]. Where the earlier Cosmocazo had produced iron-and-glass market halls, civic buildings, and cultural institutions on a human scale, the Deco phase applied the movement's multi-ethnic decorative synthesis to the [[W:Early skyscrapers|steel-frame skyscraper]] — the building type that Gilded Age industrialists, merchant families, and foreign investors adopted as the supreme expression of commercial ambition.
[[File:Chrysler Building spire, Manhattan, by Carol Highsmith (LOC highsm.04444).jpg|thumb|366x366px|The building's distinctive Art Deco crown and spire]]
The Cosmocazo Deco vocabulary was characterized by several distinctive features: [[W:Setback (architecture)|terraced setbacks]] — the stepped massing that allowed buildings to rise in a series of receding tiers, creating a visual rhythm of ascending planes; [[W:Glazed architectural terra-cotta|glazed architectural ceramics]] — the [[W:Azulejo|Andalusi tile tradition]] applied at skyscraper scale, with geometric and floral patterns in vivid colors cladding the setback facades and spandrel panels; stainless-steel ornamental crowns — sunburst arches, geometric finials, and stylized solitary eagles (the [[National symbols of Balisca|national bird]]) or corporate insignia fabricated from polished stainless steel and ornamental programs integrating geometric patterns, [[W:Arabesque|Bayani]] tilework, and indigenous cosmological motifs into a single decorative scheme.
The most celebrated example of the Cosmocazo Deco is the [[Edificio Kayani|''Edificio Kayani'']] (1917–1932), a 42-storey steel-frame tower on the [[Avenida Séptimo]] in [[Castejón]]'s [[Expansión]], commissioned by the [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani-Baliscan]] shipping magnate [[Kayani family|Daud Kayani]] as a headquarters for the [[Kayani Maritime Company]]. The building's terraced setbacks are clad in [[W:Islamic geometric patterns|Islamic geometric terracotta]], its crown of stainless-steel sunburst arches remains one of the most recognizable features of the Castejón skyline, and the Kaftian tilework added by the construction syndicates during the post-revolutionary repurposing — replacing the Kayani insignia on the setback panels — transformed the building from a corporate monument into a communal one without destroying its character.
[[File:Edificio Almodêvar.jpg|left|thumb|375x375px|[[Edificio Almodêvar]] with the industrial motifs embedded in its terracotta cladding (2025)]]
Other significant Cosmocazo Deco towers included the [[Torre del Comercio]] (1914, 32 stories), the first steel-frame skyscraper in Castejón, commissioned by the [[Hesperian League#Hesperian Company|Hesperian League]] merchant consortium; the [[Edificio Zong-Abyala]] (1916, 28 stories), the headquarters of the [[Zong-Abyala Trading Company]], notable for its Zong calligraphic frieze running the full height of the setback; and the [[Edificio Almodêvar]] (incomplete in 1919, 38 stories), commissioned by the [[Almodêvar Steel Corporation]] and distinguished by the industrial motifs incorporated into its terracotta cladding.[[File:Lobby of Edificio Kayani.jpg|thumb|The lobby of Edificio Kayani]]The Cosmocazo Deco towers were the last Baliscan buildings designed by and for the private capitalist class. When the [[Baliscan Revolution]] halted construction in 1918, several towers stood incomplete — their steel skeletons towering above the rest of the city, with their interiors unfinished, and their cranes still standing. The debate over what to do with these abandoned towers became one of the defining [[Baliscan post-revolution social policy|architectural controversies of the post-revolutionary period]], ultimately resolved through the repurposing strategy that converted them into cooperative towers with communal interiors while preserving their Gilded Age-era exteriors. The resulting architectural contraditions — capitalist shells housing communal interiors — became a defining feature of the Castejón cityscape and a physical embodiment of revolutionary [[W:Détournement|''diversión'']] - the "hijacking" of the old order.

=== Architectural legacy ===
{{Further|Baliscan socialist realism}}
The Cosmocazo's architectural legacy proved foundational for the post-revolutionary [[Baliscan socialist realism|communal realists]]. The movement's multi-ethnic decorative synthesis — its integration of Kaftian, Bayani, and indigenous ornamental traditions within a single architectural vocabulary — provided the precedent that communal realism would adapt to revolutionary purposes. Where the Cosmocazo had employed this synthesis in service of commercial and civic prestige, the communal realists redirected it toward communal assembly and collective labor, transforming the cosmopolitan aesthetic into a revolutionary one.

The Cosmocazo's critique of industrialist display — its opposition to the "gaudy displays of wealth" that characterized the Gilded Age — was taken up by the [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist faction]] during the post-revolutionary aesthetic debate, informing their position that the abandoned Gilded Age towers should be demolished rather than repurposed. The synthesis faction's counter-argument — that the steel frame was the product of workers' labor and could serve the commune as readily as the corporation — effectively extended the Cosmocazo's own logic: the movement had always sought to reconcile industrial modernity with cultural pluralism, and the repurposed towers represented the fulfillment of that aspiration under conditions the Cosmocazo's own reformers had never imagined.

